{"kind":"assert","text":"Hugh, 21 November 21:00 EST: three observations from use of the live level in the map application. Their places in the queue:\n1. A live observer in the client beside the recorded observer. This is PB-3 (a294276d). Add to PB-3: the observer parses the generation and tick events (today the application treats raw stream chunks as refresh signals), coalesces reads, shares session renewal with the recorded observer, and exposes distinct failure states: signed out, not a member, reset, scope-full, refused by a guard, transport down. A refused write is not retried on the heartbeat cadence: only transport failures and unknown answers retry, with backoff. The second half of the observation, heartbeats that spend the generation budget, is the new request LL-1, filed with this act. My 15 second renewal ruling stands for the design and makes LL-1 necessary before Pinboard runs for hours.\n2. One recorded client for demo, tests and hosted mode. The dap part: ScopeClient needs a deterministic in-memory transport that an application's demo and model tests use, backed by the real judges and fold, so an application keeps no second engine. Builder: report in one act whether the derive testing Ledger already gives this behind the client transport interface, and what is missing; I file the request from that answer. The consolidation of the update paths and the derived map data is work in the map application's checkout.\n3. Signed application releases for an application with a live companion scope. PB-2 (f6bdca65) admits the three live routes. Still missing: typed discovery of the companion live scope from the installed application, and live routing bound to the application's capability, so a screen needs no configured scope ref. I file that as a design request after PB-2 lands and LL-1 is ruled; record it in the PB-2 review as the known next step.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}